+
+| [R31-Q01] | `Device unallocated` in `btrfs filesystem usage -b /` near 0: metadata reservations stay inside the allocated metadata chunks, so ENOSPC can come while data chunks have room | Read `Device unallocated` before `df`; near 0 with full metadata is the btrfs ENOSPC-with-free-space case | S159 line 177 |
+| [R31-Q02] | `Global reserve` is metadata held for emergency work such as deletion on a full filesystem; capped at 512 MiB in v7.2 | Never count it as free space; `used` above 0 means the emergency reserve is in use `[inf]`: treat the filesystem as full | S152 line 418; S156 line 394 |
+| [R31-Q03] | `btrfs balance start -dusage=N` relocates only block groups under N percent used and returns them to unallocated space | Operator only: start with `-dusage=0`, which needs no new work space, then raise N | S153 lines 50-54 |
+| [R31-Q04] | Metadata on one device defaults to `DUP`: every metadata byte costs two raw bytes (`Metadata ratio` 2.00 here) | Budget metadata chunks at twice their logical size | S155 lines 59 and 520; `btrfs-filesystem-usage.out.txt` line 11; capture lines 53 and 67 |
+| [R31-Q05] | `allocation/<type>/bg_reclaim_threshold` is a percentage of block-group size: the v7.2 periodic sweep reclaims a whole block group whose used bytes fall below it (the chapter calls it a reclaimable-space percentage; corrected after wave-2 RevRowD05) | Read only; metadata reads 0 on this host with `dynamic_reclaim` 0, so the periodic sweep marks no metadata block group (`[inf]`; other reclaim paths `[unk]`) | S154 lines 168-169; S159 lines 2111-2117 and 2154-2155 |
+| [R31-Q06] | `/sys/fs/btrfs/<fsid>/allocation/` (singular on 7.2.11; the v7.1 chapter heading spells `allocations`) holds `global_rsv_*` and the data, metadata and system directories, world-readable | Use these files for an unprivileged capture; `btrfs filesystem usage` without root lacks chunk detail | S158 lines 37, 68-78 and 1066-1068; S154 line 140; `.ufo/waves/round-30/w1/ExecHostCapture/btrfs-filesystem-usage.err.txt` line 1 |
+| [R31-Q07] | Snapshots share extents with their source, so deleting files in the original frees nothing a snapshot still references | Deleting the snapshots that still reference the extents is what returns the space; deleting files alone cannot. Which snapshots to delete is an operator decision (this host had no snapper configuration on 2026-10-22, wave-2 RevRowD07) | S157 lines 39-41 and 202-206 |
+| [R31-Q08] | Host metadata `bytes_used` 35372056576 of `total_bytes` 38654705664 (91.5 percent) | High fill alone is not ENOSPC while unallocated space remains (D01, D09) | S154 line 163; capture lines 50, 67 and 99 |
